body:
ashok: Heads-up — after the last chart change the GPU nodeSelector helper is producing fields that break our Flux sync in eu-west. helm lint in CI passes locally but gitops apply fails with: "invalid value for nodeSelector: must be map[string]string". Anyone seen this drift between lint/templating and runtime?

ci-cd-bot: [pipeline #4821] deploy-gpu-pool (eu-west) failed during kube apply: error: error validating data: ValidationError(Deployment.spec.template.spec.nodeSelector): unknown. See details in CI logs: https://ci.redwood.internal/4821

maria: Quick glance — do we render nodeSelector when gpu.enabled=false? If helper emits `null` or `[]` helm can output bad types. A snippet would help.

ashok: Paste of helper and values below:
```yaml
# values.yaml
nodeSelector:
  gpu: "nvidia-tesla-v100"
  gpuCount: 1

# templates/_helpers.tpl
{{- define "gpu.nodeSelector" -}}
{{- if .Values.nodeSelector }}
nodeSelector: {{ toYaml .Values.nodeSelector | nindent 8 }}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
```

jules: I think the root is that `nodeSelector` is expected to be a map at the API level, but your helper is injecting a top-level `nodeSelector:` key inside a block that itself gets inserted under `spec.template.spec` — double-keying can lead to Helm merging weirdly. Also `toYaml` will render "nvidia-tesla-v100" correctly but if some env sets `nodeSelector: null` upstream, `toYaml` makes `null` literal which becomes invalid.

devex: Two fast mitigations: 1) Ensure the helper only emits the map contents (no `nodeSelector:` key) and let the parent template write the key. 2) Add a values.schema.json so `helm lint --values` or `helm template --validate` will catch type mismatches in CI. Example schema: 
```json
{
  "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "nodeSelector": {"type": ["object","null"], "additionalProperties": {"type": "string"}},
    "gpuCount": {"type": "integer", "minimum": 0}
  }
}
```

maria: Also be careful with Helm's `--validate` in CI — it uses the cluster's apiserver validation which can be stricter than local lint. For Flux/GitOps we should run `helm template` in the pipeline and then `kubectl apply --server-dry-run=client --validate=true` against a compliance cluster to catch runtime schema issues early.

ashok: Nice. Quick patch: updated helper to: 
```tpl
{{- define "gpu.nodeSelector" -}}
{{- if and .Values.nodeSelector (not (eq .Values.nodeSelector nil)) }}
{{- range $k, $v := .Values.nodeSelector }}
  {{ $k }}: "{{ $v }}"
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
```
And parent template inserts `nodeSelector:` above it. Pushed to branch, triggered CI.

infra-bot: [alert] drift-detection job:manifest-diff found differences for cluster us-west: nodeSelector key present in live but missing in desired for deployment/gpu-daemon.

jules: One more gotcha — our helm lint job uses `kubeversion:1.25` in tests, but prod clusters in us-west are 1.27 and validate against newer CRD schemas. We should unify lint matrix to include latest minor to avoid false negatives.

devex: Agreed. Proposal: add a small matrix to CI: helm lint + helm template against k8s 1.25, 1.27, and a server-validate step for the region where the change will roll out first. I can add a reusable job in .gitlab-ci that other charts can consume.

maria: Also consider adding a `values-ci.yaml` override for GPU pools to ensure CI uses `nodeSelector: {}` explicitly rather than leaving it null. That catches the null-vs-empty-map issue.

ashok: CI passed after change, flux resynced in eu-west. Leaving PR open for the schema+matrix tweaks. Thanks all :thumbsup:

devex: I'll open a follow-up PR with the CI job template and a docs blurb for chart authors on safe helpers + values.schema.json.

ashok: filing a short postmortem in #postmortems with links to PRs and the manifest diff so infra ops can audit which clusters saw the drift.

ci-cd-bot: [pipeline #4821] deploy-gpu-pool (eu-west) succeeded on retry.
